  "control_correction": {
    "defect": "A first pass reported 'mismatched profile still beats no profile (flat q3) in 0/6 cells'. That comparison is at UNEQUAL COST and is exactly the error this campaign keeps flagging: flat q3 is 3.25 bits and 0.8521 ps/element while planes k2 is 2.50 bits and 0.6676 ps/element. A dearer codec having lower error is not a verdict about profiles.",
    "corrected": "The profile-dependent codec is now BRACKETED by two profile-independent flat codecs, one cheaper and one dearer, so its position is readable.",
    "planes_k2_beats_cheaper_flat_q2_cells": 6,
    "cells": 6
  },
  "verdict": {
    "profile_barely_matters": "A mismatched profile costs +2.91% over a matched one (max +8.85%), and the POOLED profile -- the general build -- costs only +0.44% (max +0.92%). Activation statistics transfer between these workloads almost completely.",
    "consequence_for_the_obligation": "Profile-guided compilation is REAL but SMALL on this axis. A separate HAWKING-WORKLOAD build would recover under 3% of functional error over the general build, and the general pooled build gives up under 0.5% against an oracle it could never have. The narrowing a specialized build must declare is therefore small enough that keeping ONE general build is the better engineering choice on current evidence.",
    "consequence_for_F10": "This substantially DE-RATES F10. Every d_j-weighted result in this campaign was fitted on prose, and the measured cost of that mismatch on the one contrasting workload available is under 3%. F10's qualifier stands -- the statistics are prose statistics -- but the expected error from it is small, not order-of-magnitude. The untested classes (math, multilingual, tool) could still behave differently and this does not speak for them.",
    "scope": "prose against code, at one layer per organ, three organs. The contrast that would stress this hardest (math, tool syntax) has no captured rows."
  }
